-# TINYML-ESP32-SAFETY-RELAY
+# TinyML ESP32 Safety Relay
+
 > **Category:** Edge AI  
+> **Platform adapter:** `m25-tinyml-safety`  
+> **Status:** integrated  
+> **Collection position:** model **22** of 25 (alphabetical)
+
+<!-- Generated from deployment/models.catalog.json - edit the catalog, not this file. -->
+
+A depth-5 decision tree compiled to C++ and dropped onto an ESP32, plus a mirrored in-loop gate that trips on the same hard limits before SCADA is ever written. It is the only model allowed to say STOP, and the only one whose failure mode is physical.
+
+## How this page fits the collection
+
+The WT-PM collection numbers its 25 repositories **alphabetically 01-25** - that is the number on this page - and gives each one a **task-based adapter id** (`m25-tinyml-safety`) used by the platform registry, the `wt-pm` CLI and the fusion engine. Both are printed on every page so the ecosystem graph and the registry can be read against each other.
+
+- Repository: <https://github.com/rajaram-2005/wt-pm-tinyml-esp32-safety-relay>
+- Landing page: <https://rajaram-2005.github.io/wt-pm-tinyml-esp32-safety-relay/>
+- Ecosystem graph: <https://rajaram-2005.github.io/wt-pm-lstm-scada-anomaly/ecosystem.html>
+- Platform architecture: <https://github.com/rajaram-2005/wt-pm-lstm-scada-anomaly/blob/main/ARCHITECTURE.md>
+- Per-model table: <https://github.com/rajaram-2005/wt-pm-lstm-scada-anomaly/blob/main/platform/docs/MODELS.md>
+
+## Model
+
+- `DecisionTree classifier in model.py`
+- `micromlgen export producing model.h for the ESP32 firmware`
+
+| Block | Detail |
+|---|---|
+| Model | Depth-5 decision tree over bearing_vib_rms_mm_s, gearbox_oil_temp_c, rotor_speed_rpm and power_kw |
+| Export | model.h C++ header generated by micromlgen, compiled into bare-metal firmware |
+| Mirror | The SafetyManager re-evaluates the same limits in-process, so the platform's answer and the rail's answer cannot diverge silently |
+
+## Advanced concepts
+
+The ideas this model actually leans on - stated with their failure modes, not as marketing.
+
+### Why a five-level tree wins
+
+On an ESP32 the constraint is not accuracy, it is deterministic 50 ms latency with no allocator and no floating-point surprise. A depth-5 tree is a handful of comparisons - auditable, certifiable and impossible to make non-deterministic.
+
+### Compiled, not served
+
+micromlgen emits C++, so the model stops being an artifact and becomes firmware. That is a different trust boundary from every other model here: no dependency can go missing at runtime because there is no runtime.
+
+### Two implementations, one truth
+
+The C++ tree and the in-loop SafetyManager evaluate the same limits. Divergence between them is a detectable fault of its own - the platform reports the mirrored decision so an operator can see whether the rail and the cloud agree.
+
+### Safety is not a vote
 
-## Description
-C++ exported TinyML decision trees for bare-metal execution on ESP32 microcontrollers triggering immediate safety trips.
+m25 never enters the fusion weighting. It is excluded from the ensemble by construction: an average of opinions is not a protection function.
 
-## Structure
+## Platform contract
+
+| | |
+|---|---|
+| Input (adapter view) | safety channels - bearing_vib_rms_mm_s, gearbox_oil_temp_c, rotor_speed_rpm, power_kw |
+| Output (`WTDataSchema`) | prediction (trip / continue) + extra['model_h'] |
+| Record format | `wt-pm.platform.v1` |
+| Typical latency | 50 ms |
+| Deployment targets | cloud, mcu, edge_cpu |
+| Resource requirements | scikit-learn, micromlgen (for C++ export) |
+| Fallback | none - this model is a leaf |
+| Subsystem focus | whole turbine |
+| Hermes tool | `safety` |
+
+Records follow the shared `WTDataSchema` (`wt-pm.platform.v1`): `timestamp`, `turbine_id` and `model_id` are required; every other field is optional, so a model fills in only what it honestly produces and the fusion layer can tell absence from a zero.
+
+## Platform layers
+
+| Layer | What this model does there |
+|---|---|
+| **Safety gate** | Hard limits and trips; the only model permitted to veto, and excluded from fusion weighting |
+| **Hermes** | safety tool - the SAFE answer in the agent's summary |
+| **Edge** | The only model declaring the MCU deployment target; the router categorically refuses to send any other model there |
+| **Audit** | Its decisions are recorded in the DecisionAuditLog alongside the analysis that produced them |
+
+Layers are described once, in the catalog, and referenced by every model that participates in them: fusion, calibrated uncertainty, drift and data trust, explainability, physics constraints, the safety gate, the evaluation protocol and the Hermes agent.
+
+## How it connects to the other 24
+
+**Receives from**
+
+- **25. XGBoost Tabular Faults** (`m11-xgboost-tabular`) - distilled depth-5 tree exported to the ESP32 relay
+- **18. Random Forest Telemetry** (`m10-random-forest`) - safety-trip rules compiled from the forest
+
+Related pages: [18 Random Forest](https://github.com/rajaram-2005/wt-pm-random-forest-telemetry), [25 XGBoost Tabular](https://github.com/rajaram-2005/wt-pm-xgboost-tabular-faults)
+
