+<h1>TinyML ESP32 Safety Relay</h1>
+<p class="lede">A depth-5 decision tree compiled to C++ and dropped onto an ESP32, plus a mirrored in-loop gate that trips on the same hard limits before SCADA is ever written. It is the only model allowed to say STOP, and the only one whose failure mode is physical.</p>
+<div class="cta">
+<a class="btn" href="https://github.com/rajaram-2005/wt-pm-tinyml-esp32-safety-relay">View on GitHub</a>
+<a class="btn ghost" href="https://rajaram-2005.github.io/wt-pm-lstm-scada-anomaly/ecosystem.html">25-model ecosystem graph</a>
+<a class="btn ghost" href="https://github.com/rajaram-2005/wt-pm-lstm-scada-anomaly/blob/main/ARCHITECTURE.md">Platform architecture</a>
+</div>
+</header>
+<p class="note">This page is generated from <code>deployment/models.catalog.json</code> in the reference repository, together with the other 24 model pages, so all 25 agree on numbering, contract and status.</p>
+<h2>Model</h2>
+<ul>
+<li><code>DecisionTree classifier in model.py</code></li>
+<li><code>micromlgen export producing model.h for the ESP32 firmware</code></li>
+</ul>
+<table>
+<tr><th>Model</th><td>Depth-5 decision tree over bearing_vib_rms_mm_s, gearbox_oil_temp_c, rotor_speed_rpm and power_kw</td></tr>
+<tr><th>Export</th><td>model.h C++ header generated by micromlgen, compiled into bare-metal firmware</td></tr>
+<tr><th>Mirror</th><td>The SafetyManager re-evaluates the same limits in-process, so the platform's answer and the rail's answer cannot diverge silently</td></tr>
+</table>
+<h2>Advanced concepts</h2>
+<p class="note">The ideas this model actually leans on, each stated with its failure mode.</p>
+<div class="card">
+<h3>Why a five-level tree wins</h3>
+<p>On an ESP32 the constraint is not accuracy, it is deterministic 50 ms latency with no allocator and no floating-point surprise. A depth-5 tree is a handful of comparisons - auditable, certifiable and impossible to make non-deterministic.</p>
+</div>
+<div class="card">
+<h3>Compiled, not served</h3>
+<p>micromlgen emits C++, so the model stops being an artifact and becomes firmware. That is a different trust boundary from every other model here: no dependency can go missing at runtime because there is no runtime.</p>
+</div>
+<div class="card">
+<h3>Two implementations, one truth</h3>
+<p>The C++ tree and the in-loop SafetyManager evaluate the same limits. Divergence between them is a detectable fault of its own - the platform reports the mirrored decision so an operator can see whether the rail and the cloud agree.</p>
+</div>
+<div class="card">
+<h3>Safety is not a vote</h3>
+<p>m25 never enters the fusion weighting. It is excluded from the ensemble by construction: an average of opinions is not a protection function.</p>
+</div>
+<h2>Platform contract</h2>
+<table>
+<tr><th>Input (adapter view)</th><td>safety channels - bearing_vib_rms_mm_s, gearbox_oil_temp_c, rotor_speed_rpm, power_kw</td></tr>
+<tr><th>Output</th><td>prediction (trip / continue) + extra['model_h']</td></tr>
+<tr><th>Record format</th><td><code>wt-pm.platform.v1</code></td></tr>
+<tr><th>Typical latency</th><td>50 ms</td></tr>
+<tr><th>Deployment targets</th><td>cloud, mcu, edge_cpu</td></tr>
+<tr><th>Requirements</th><td>scikit-learn, micromlgen (for C++ export)</td></tr>
+<tr><th>Fallback</th><td>none - leaf model</td></tr>
+<tr><th>Subsystem focus</th><td>whole turbine</td></tr>
+<tr><th>Hermes tool</th><td><code>safety</code></td></tr>
+</table>
+<p class="note">Records follow the shared <code>WTDataSchema</code> (<code>wt-pm.platform.v1</code>). Only <code>timestamp</code>, <code>turbine_id</code> and <code>model_id</code> are required; absence is distinguishable from a zero.</p>
+<h2>Platform layers</h2>
+<table>
+<tr><th>Safety gate</th><td>Hard limits and trips; the only model permitted to veto, and excluded from fusion weighting</td></tr>
+<tr><th>Hermes</th><td>safety tool - the SAFE answer in the agent's summary</td></tr>
+<tr><th>Edge</th><td>The only model declaring the MCU deployment target; the router categorically refuses to send any other model there</td></tr>
+<tr><th>Audit</th><td>Its decisions are recorded in the DecisionAuditLog alongside the analysis that produced them</td></tr>
+</table>
+<h2>How it connects to the other 24</h2>
+<ul class="edges">
+<li><b>25. XGBoost Tabular Faults</b> <span class="note">(m11-xgboost-tabular)</span> &rarr; distilled depth-5 tree exported to the ESP32 relay</li>
+<li><b>18. Random Forest Telemetry</b> <span class="note">(m10-random-forest)</span> &rarr; safety-trip rules compiled from the forest</li>
+</ul>
+<h2>Honest limits</h2><ul class="limits">
+<li>Fixed depth-5 structure - it cannot express a fault it was not shaped for</li>
+<li>Limits are configuration, not derived from a certified safety case; this is a research demo, not a SIL-rated protection system</li>
+</ul>
+<p class="note">Fidelity: rung 1 of the fidelity ladder - simulator data, surrogate waveforms where stated, proxy RUL targets, simulator fault labels. Comparable within this collection, not field-validated turbine control.</p>
